                                                  \begin{document}$$Z \in \left\{-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\},$$\end{document}and we fit a linear model using all pairwise outcome differences and average moderator values across pairs of treated and control participants [13]. After estimating the weights for each effect moderator included in the model, we simulated testing datasets following the same specifications as the training sets but with a different replication seed, allowing evaluation of the PTR on independent data to reduce overfitting. In simulating the testing datasets, participants would be randomly assigned to either treatment or control arm. In the same replicate, while keeping all else the same, we switched the treatment assignment to generate a counterfactual outcome. This allows us to define an individual’s optimal strategy (i.e., treatment yielding the larger EWTR) and to evaluate PTR performance relative to this benchmark, which is not observable in practice. Then, we used the same process to derive EWTR for each participant, obtaining the expected outcome if all participants were assigned to the control strategy and the expected outcome if all participants were assigned to the treatment strategy. Using the weights previously estimated in the training data, we compute the composite moderator M* for each participant in the testing set. We fit a linear model on the subject-level with the EWTR as the outcome, treatment (Z), composite moderator (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
