sec.p:
For each scenario, we assessed the following four performance measures. We report on the empirical rejection rate, the proportion of simulated trials in which the treatment × composite moderator M* interaction coefficient was non-zero at α =0.05 with EWTR as the outcome in the testing dataset. The rejection rate is the type I error for null effect modification scenarios and in alternative scenarios, it is the power to detect effect modification. The misclassification rate is computed at the subject level by comparing the PTR to each subject’s optimal strategy, defined as the strategy with the larger EWTR from the EWTRs simulated under both control and treatment arms. In other words, it is the proportion of the simulated testing set patients in which the PTR conflicts with the patient’s known optimal strategy. We summarized the expected outcomes by averaging EWTR across the test set under the three strategies: all received treatment, all received control, and PTR. For the expected PTR, we averaged the EWTR from the arm recommended for each subject. Lastly, we considered the gain from personalization, or expected change in the outcome under a PTR compared to a strategy where all participants received treatment [4]:16\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${\Theta}_{Treatment}= \mu \left(PTR\right)-\mu \left(Treatment\right)$$\end{document}and analogously compared to where all participants received control:17\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${\Theta}_{Control}= \mu \left(PTR\right)-\mu \left(Control\right).$$\end{document}

In the alternative scenarios, Fig. 2 illustrates the increasing power with higher ratio magnitude (stronger effect modification) for each moderator and the composite moderator M*. When the effect modification was substantial, the composite moderator M* consistently yielded similar power to the strongest moderator, or the highest power relative to the individual effect moderators. In Scenarios 2 and 4, which included strong qualitative moderators, the power (≥ 0.70) for both the strongest individual and composite moderator M* rapidly increased at a ratio magnitude Φ ≥ 1. In Scenario 5, all individual moderators gradually increased in power with higher ratio magnitude Φ. Notably, the composite moderator M* had a steep increase in power at a ratio magnitude Φ ≥ 1 and a shift from more quantitative to qualitative interaction patterns. For Scenario 3, which was largely absent of qualitative interactions, the power for all moderators increased modestly with ratio magnitudes and the composite moderator M* generally outperformed the individual moderators.Fig. 2
