Expected change in outcome under a PTR strategy compared to all-on-treatment strategy across ratio magnitudes of effect modification for alternative scenarios. Boxplots show the distribution of the gains from personalization or expected change in outcome under PTR versus all-on-treatment strategy for 1000 simulated trials (N = 1000). Black dotted line marks the change at zero. Columns left to right increase in the treatment-moderator interaction-to-main effect ratio. Each row represents a scenario. Blue-gray boxplots are individual moderators, and yellow boxplots are the composite moderators M*

We re-analyzed the trial data incorporating the first adverse event (AE) event (e.g., arrhythmia, ischemic stroke, major bleeding, etc.) as a clinical state, in addition to death. Figure 5 illustrates the clinical state probability distribution over the common follow-up for the rate-control strategy (reference) and the distribution of the EWTR between the two treatment strategies. Most participants remained in the initial state, and we observe a higher probability of death relative to non-fatal AE over the duration of follow-up. In a linear model for EWTR comparing treatment strategies, rate-control had slightly more excess days in a better clinical state relative to rhythm-control (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
