                                                  \begin{document}$${M}_{intersection}^{*}=24.2$$\end{document} and this is within the observed range. The intersection represents the split in the two subgroups, if subjects have M* ≥ 24.2 then under PTR they would be recommended the rhythm-control strategy and if subjects have M* < 24.2 they would be recommended the rate-control strategy. In cross-validation analyses, the treatment × M* interaction was significant (p < 0.05). Using the Gail–Simon test, we found insufficient evidence of qualitative interaction on EWTR (p = 0.06 for 4 strata; p = 0.10 for 3 strata). The results instead suggested a quantitative interaction, with consistent treatment effect direction but varying magnitude across levels of M*.

The expected outcome recommending all participants on rhythm-control was worse in EWTR relative to rate-control (−40.7 [95% bootstrap CI −70.3 to −16.2] vs −19.2 [95% bootstrap CI −46.9 to 6.0] days). The gains in personalization for the single and composite moderators were noticeably higher against the all-on-rhythm-control strategy but were minimal relative to the all-on-rate-control strategy (Fig. 6). Compared to a single moderator, using the composite moderator performed the best under a PTR strategy and had the highest gains in personalization relative to all-on-rhythm or all-on-rate control strategies.Fig. 6
